- C) 15%
- D) 51%

@answer

Answer\: A

=== layout: one-panel

# Human genome abundanceof non-coding DNA

@body

- 98.5% “junk” DNA
- 1.5% protein-coding genes

=== layout: one-panel

# Human genome abundanceof non-coding DNA

@body

- 25.9% introns
- 20.4% long interspersed nuclear elements \[LINES\]
- 13.1% short interspersed nuclear elements \[SINES\]
- 11.6% miscellaneous unique sequences
- 8.3% long terminal repeats \[LTR\] retrotransposons
- 8% miscellaneous compacted DNA or heterochromatin
- 5% segmental duplications
- 3% simple sequence repeats
- 2.9% DNA transposons
- 1.5% protein-coding genes

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Slide image 31](assets/lect02c-genome_sizes/image_031.jpg)

=== layout: section

# Summary

=== layout: one-panel

# Summary

@body

- Complexity ≠ # of Genes
- More complex organisms do not always have larger genomes than simpler ones.
  - Any given genome seems to contain more DNA than would be needed for the predicted gene number
- Some closely related species exhibit divergent DNA contents.

=== layout: one-panel

